; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Planet Orbits
; CATEGORY: Science & Simulation
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     gravity, orbits, newton, multiple turtles, property lists
; SUMMARY:  Three planets and a comet circle a sun, moved only by Newton's law of gravity in tiny time steps.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   A yellow sun sits in the middle of a starry sky. Three planets
;   and a comet, each one its own turtle, trace out their orbits.
;   Nobody tells them to go round in circles: every tick the sun pulls
;   each one a little, and the curved paths appear by themselves.
;   The comet starts too slow for a circle, so it dives close to the
;   sun, whips around fast and swings far out again.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run and watch. Press Stop when you have seen enough.
;   Try changing the starting speeds in the MAKE.BODY lines.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * Newton's law: the pull gets weaker with distance squared
;   * stepping a simulation: speed changes position, force changes speed
;   * property lists as little records (PPROP and GPROP)
;   * several turtles, one per object, with SETTURTLE
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Give a planet the speed SQRT (:gm / radius) for a perfect circle.
;   * Make the comet a little faster and see whether it escapes.
;   * Make :dt bigger. When does the simulation stop being accurate?
; ============================================================

; GM is the strength of the sun's gravity (G times the sun's mass).
make "gm 40000
make "dt 0.05
make "bodies []

; Each body is a property list holding its turtle number, position
; and velocity. A property list is like a card with labeled boxes.
to make.body :name :turtleno :color :x :y :vx :vy
  pprop :name "turtle :turtleno
  pprop :name "x :x  pprop :name "y :y
  pprop :name "vx :vx  pprop :name "vy :vy
  make "bodies lput :name :bodies
  setturtle :turtleno
  penup setxy :x :y setpc :color setpensize 2
  setshape "circle showturtle pendown
end

; One small step of time for one body.
to move.body :name
  localmake "x gprop :name "x
  localmake "y gprop :name "y
  localmake "r2 :x * :x + :y * :y
  localmake "r sqrt :r2
  ; The pull is GM divided by distance squared, aimed at the sun.
  ; Dividing x and y by r turns them into a direction one step long.
  localmake "pull :gm / :r2
  localmake "ax (minus :pull) * :x / :r
  localmake "ay (minus :pull) * :y / :r
  ; Change the speed first and then the position (the Euler-Cromer
  ; method). Doing it the other way round makes orbits slowly spiral out.
  localmake "vx (gprop :name "vx) + :ax * :dt
  localmake "vy (gprop :name "vy) + :ay * :dt
  pprop :name "vx :vx  pprop :name "vy :vy
  pprop :name "x :x + :vx * :dt
  pprop :name "y :y + :vy * :dt
  setturtle gprop :name "turtle
  setxy gprop :name "x gprop :name "y
end

to draw.sky
  setbackground 0
  setpc 7
  repeat 120 [penup setxy (random 800) - 400 (random 600) - 300 dot pos]
  ; The sun: 36 short sides make a circle of radius 25. Starting 25 to
  ; the left of the middle and heading up keeps it centered.
  penup setxy -25 0 setheading 0 pendown
  setpc 14 setpensize 1
  filled 6 [repeat 36 [fd 4.4 rt 10]]
  penup setxy 0 0 hideturtle
end

to run.sky :ticks
  repeat :ticks [
    foreach :bodies [move.body ?]
    wait 1
  ]
end

clearscreen
setturtle 0
draw.sky
; name, turtle, color, starting x and y, starting vx and vy
; For a circle the speed is SQRT (:gm / radius): 20 for radius 100.
make.body "mercury 1 15 100 0 0 20
make.body "earth 2 1 180 0 0 14.9
make.body "mars 3 4 0 -260 12.4 0
; The comet is much slower than a circle at this distance would need.
make.body "comet 4 11 -330 0 0 -6
run.sky 4000
